  "description": "Shenora's Chromium engine, through CEF, for an app that ships its own browser engine instead of WebView2. UseChromium is a shell of the kit's own, and a complete one: an app on Windows, macOS or Linux needs no other shell package. A splash the app composes in C#, drawn natively in the main window's render area while Chromium starts, with the window's own frame live around it (and, optionally, a card before the window exists), lifted once the page is ready. Its page comes from a folder or from resources embedded in the app's assembly, with a page of the app's own for a page load that finds nothing. The app's colour scheme, light, dark or the OS's, set on Chromium itself, so the page's prefers-color-scheme and Chrome's own UI follow it. CEF's windows with frameless chrome (real caption buttons and Snap Layouts on Windows), file drops with real paths, native file dialogs, the clipboard, a tray, one instance per install that hands a later launch's arguments to the running app (on macOS the files and links it is opened with too), a main window that opens where it was left (with WindowState set), a window's system animations on or off, a native launcher's startup screen closed once the app's first window is on screen, secondary windows, revealing files and launching processes, naming who holds a file open, the app's unhandled exceptions, the main window's input taken while something modal runs, crash reload and dev-server hot reload, with IPC over a transport that needs no code in the renderer. It hosts the auxiliary browser sessions too: windowless browsers for the pool and streaming, and a window of its own for an interactive session. ChromiumEngine and ChromiumChildBrowser embed a page in a window the host owns, which is how Shenora.Windows hosts one beside its WebView2 control. ChromiumBrowserProcess runs Chromium as a browser of its own, in a second process of the app's executable: Chromium's own windows and a debugging port for an agent's tools, with no app page in reach of it. CEF itself is not in the package: the app's build fetches the pinned CEF build, checks its SHA-1, and lays the app out, on Windows beside CEF's launcher with the kit's shim, which is what gives Chromium its sandbox (a Windows publish can instead keep three files at its root, with CEF in engine\\ and the app in lib\\: ShenoraChromiumPublishFolders), and on macOS as an app bundle whose helpers sandbox each subprocess. Reference it from the app's own project, with a RuntimeIdentifier and an assembly named <App>.App. Windows (x64, arm64), macOS (x64, arm64), where the build makes an app bundle, and Linux (x64, arm64), where it lays the app out beside the kit's helper, which every Chromium subprocess runs.",
